SIMPLE EXPLANATION OF THIS PROJECT
==================================

What is this project?
---------------------
This project is a virtual laboratory for studying electricity distribution networks. It is called a digital twin because it behaves like a simplified computer model of a real power system.

Instead of working on a live grid, users can run a simulation that shows how a feeder behaves over time.

What it helps people do
-----------------------
The project helps users:
- understand how electricity flows through a feeder,
- study how different customer types consume power,
- test meter behavior and communication issues,
- simulate non-technical losses such as theft or bypassing,
- separate those losses from operational problems like load shedding,
- and generate charts, reports, and analysis-ready datasets that are easy to explain.

Why it matters
--------------
Electricity utilities need to understand where losses come from and how unusual events affect the network. This project creates a safe, controlled environment for exploring those ideas without needing access to confidential utility data.

What the simulation includes
----------------------------
The current version can:
- build a feeder model with IEEE test cases,
- create synthetic customer load patterns,
- assign smart and legacy meters,
- simulate measurement errors and communication loss,
- add load-shedding or outage-type events,
- inject NTL-style scenarios,
- and export results to CSV and image files, including realism, sensitivity, and labeling-ready outputs.

Why this version is relevant to South Africa
-------------------------------------------
This version was shaped to be more relevant to South African conditions. It includes examples of:
- different customer classes,
- sector-specific demand behavior,
- operational disturbances such as load shedding,
- and a clearer separation between theft-related losses and ordinary network disturbances.

How the project is organized
----------------------------
The code is split into separate parts so each task has a clear role:
- one part starts the workflow,
- one part generates load profiles,
- one part simulates meter behavior,
- one part injects NTL and operational events,
- one part runs the simulation and exports results,
- and one part creates the dashboard.

How realistic is it?
---------------------
The model is still based on synthetic and benchmark-style data, so it is not yet a full replacement for real utility operations. However, it is realistic enough to support research, education, presentation, and early-stage analytics workflows.

One-line summary
----------------
This project is a practical virtual power-system simulator for learning about feeder behavior, metering quality, and NTL analysis in a South Africa-inspired context, with exportable reports for research and demonstration use.
